{"version":2,"text":"Text B\n\nAssessment\n\nFocus on evidence of envenomation.\n\n• Once the possibility of snakebite has been raised, it is important to determine whether a child\nhas been envenomed to establish the need for antivenom.\n• This is usually done taking into consideration the combination of circumstances, symptoms,\nexamination and laboratory test results.\n• Most people bitten by snakes in Australia do not become significantly envenomed.\n\nHistory and Examination\n\nCircumstances\n\nSymptoms\n\nExaminations\n\n- Confirmed or witnessed\nbite versus suspicion that\nbite might have occurred\nText C\n\n- Headache\n\n- Evidence of a bite/ multiple\n\n- Diaphoresis\n\nbites\n\n- Nausea or vomiting\n\n- Evidence of venom movement\n\n- Abdominal pain\n\n(e.g. sowllen or tender draining\n\n- Diarrhoea\n\nlymph nodes)\n\n- Blurred or double vision\n\n- Neurotix paralysis (ptosis,\n\n- Slurring of speech\n\nophthalmoplegia, diplopia,\n\n- Muscle weakness\n\ndysarthria, limb weakness,\n\n- Respiratory distress\n\nrespiratory muscle weakness)\n\n- Bleeding from the bite site or\n\n- Coagulopathy (bleeding gums,\n\nelsewhere\n\nprolongued bleeding from\n\n- Passing dark or red urine\n\nvenepuncture sites or other\n\n- Local pain or swelling at bite\n\nwounds, including bite site)\n\nsite\n\n- Muscle damage (muscle\n\n- Muscle pain\n\ntenderness, pain on movement\n\n- Pain in lymph nodes draining\n\nweakness, dark or red urine\n\nthe bite area\n\nindicating myoglobinuria)\n\n- Were there multiple bites?\n- When?\n- Where?\n- First aid?\n- Past history?\n- Medications?\n- Allergies?\n\n- Loss of consciousness/collapse\n\nand/or convulsions\n\nPage 369","layout":{"version":1,"width":595.276001,"height":841.890015,"blocks":[{"text":"Text B","lines":[{"text":"Text B","x":0.07473222492636655,"y":0.12549562664667072,"width":0.05009440990381871,"height":0.01713287928708836,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Text","x":0.07473222492636655,"width":0.03489473784447092,"confide